Z||000000||BAM-BAM||05-14-2000||09:24 PM||||  Both my Chiltons and Haynes manuals say that you MUST replace the upper and lower control arms if it's necessary to replace the ball joints. Is this true? If so, then why do they sell them seperately? They say that the lower one is welded in. If you do not replace the whole control arm, how do you replace the ball joint? They also say the same for the bushings; if they are worn the you MUST replace the control arm. I don't get it. HELP!!!<P> Thanx, BAM-BAM  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||5
Z||000001||TOM||05-14-2000||10:05 PM||||Its not true and you need to take both those manuals and chuck them out on the freeway and get the factory manual||||reg||1
Z||000002||Fiasco||05-14-2000||10:25 PM||||So they aren't welded in, then?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phil||05-14-2000||10:28 PM||||There may be a tack weld on the lowers -easy to cut||||reg||1
Z||000004||Oreif||05-14-2000||10:49 PM||||Upper ball joints (if original) are rivoted in. Drill out rivots and replace with the MOOG ball joints with nuts/bolts. The MOOG's are slotted which aids in a more adjustment during alignment. The lower ball joints are tac welded. Grind off welds and press them out. The new ones need to be pressed in and have a saftey clip that goes on top. As for bushings. If replacing with poly you will need to keep the outer sleeves. There are many ways to remove bushings. Do a search on bushings for the many ways. Just redid my entire suspension over the winter and it's not that difficult to do.||||reg||7
